#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:5
msgid "Optimization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:7
msgid ""
"A crawl goes as fast as its slowest part allows. :ref:`Find out which part "
"that is <optimize-bottleneck>` before changing any setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:10
msgid ""
":ref:`Broad crawls <broad-crawls>` have their own set of recommended "
"adjustments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:16
msgid "Finding the bottleneck"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:18
msgid ""
"The bottleneck depends on the spider: on the same machine, one crawl can be "
"limited by its own parsing code and another by the target website. So "
"measure the crawl that you want to optimize."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:22
msgid ""
":class:`~scrapy.extensions.logstats.LogStats` reports crawl speed every :"
"setting:`LOGSTATS_INTERVAL` seconds:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:25
msgid ""
"[scrapy.extensions.logstats] INFO: Crawled 1200 pages (at 60 pages/min), "
"scraped 1150 items (at 58 items/min)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:29
msgid ""
"A rate that stays flat as you raise :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS` means "
"something else is the limit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:34
msgid "Reading the engine status"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:36
msgid ""
"The :ref:`telnet console <topics-telnetconsole>` reports, through ``est()``, "
"what every part of the engine is doing at a given moment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:39
msgid ""
"len(engine.downloader.active)                   : 16\n"
"len(engine._slot.scheduler.mqs)                 : 92\n"
"len(engine.scraper.slot.active)                 : 0\n"
"engine.scraper.slot.active_size                 : 0\n"
"engine.scraper.slot.needs_backout()             : False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:47
msgid "Take a few readings at different points of the crawl:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:49
msgid ""
"``len(engine.downloader.active)`` stays at :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS`: "
"the downloader is the limit. You are waiting on the network or on the target "
"website. See :ref:`optimize-concurrency`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:53
msgid ""
"``len(engine.downloader.active)`` stays below :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS` "
"while the scheduler queues (``mqs``, ``dqs``) hold requests: something "
"throttles those requests before they reach the downloader, usually :setting:"
"`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS_PER_DOMAIN`, :setting:`DOWNLOAD_DELAY` or :ref:"
"`AutoThrottle <topics-autothrottle>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:59
msgid ""
"Both the downloader and the scheduler queues stay near empty: your spider is "
"not producing requests fast enough. A crawl that walks pagination one page "
"at a time cannot use more concurrency than it creates. See :ref:`optimize-"
"requests`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:64
msgid ""
"``needs_backout()`` is ``True``, or ``active_size`` approaches :setting:"
"`SCRAPER_SLOT_MAX_ACTIVE_SIZE`: responses arrive faster than your callbacks "
"and :ref:`item pipelines <topics-item-pipeline>` handle them. The bottleneck "
"is your own code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:69
msgid ""
"``len(engine._slot.scheduler.mqs)`` grows without settling: the crawl "
"discovers requests faster than it downloads them. This is what makes long "
"crawls run out of memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:75
msgid "Reading resource usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:77
msgid "CPU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:78
msgid ""
"Scrapy runs in a single process, and everything except DNS resolution and "
"code you explicitly move to a thread runs in a single thread. One CPU core "
"is the ceiling; a process sitting at 100% of a core is CPU-bound no matter "
"how many cores the machine has."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:83
msgid ""
"Use a sampling profiler, such as py-spy_, to find out which code is spending "
"that CPU. :ref:`Selectors <topics-selectors>` and item pipelines are the "
"usual answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:89
msgid "Memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:90
msgid ""
"The :ref:`memory usage extension <topics-extensions-ref-memusage>` records :"
"stat:`memusage/startup` and :stat:`memusage/max`. A :stat:`memusage/max` far "
"above :stat:`memusage/startup` is expected; what matters is whether it keeps "
"growing for as long as the crawl runs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:95
msgid ""
"Growth that tracks ``len(engine._slot.scheduler.mqs)`` is a scheduling "
"problem, covered in :ref:`optimize-memory`. Growth that does not is a :ref:"
"`memory leak <topics-leaks>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:99
msgid "Network"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:100
msgid ""
"Compare :stat:`downloader/response_bytes` over the crawl time against your "
"available bandwidth. Saturated bandwidth caps concurrency regardless of any "
"setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:104
msgid ""
"DNS resolution is separate: it runs on a thread pool of :setting:"
"`REACTOR_THREADPOOL_MAXSIZE` threads, and results are cached (:setting:"
"`DNSCACHE_ENABLED`, :setting:`DNSCACHE_SIZE`). It only becomes a limit of "
"its own when there are many different domains to resolve, as in :ref:`broad "
"crawls <broad-crawls>`, where it shows up as slow starts and DNS timeouts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:111
msgid "Disk"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:112
msgid ""
":ref:`Feed exports <topics-feed-exports>` write to disk on most crawls, "
"although item data is usually small enough for that not to matter. The ones "
"to suspect are :class:`~scrapy.downloadermiddlewares.httpcache."
"HttpCacheMiddleware` and the :ref:`media pipelines <topics-media-pipeline>`, "
"which write whole responses, and :setting:`JOBDIR`, which writes every "
"scheduled request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:123
msgid "Sending more requests at a time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:125
msgid ""
":setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS` caps how many requests are being downloaded "
"at any given moment, :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS_PER_DOMAIN` caps how many "
"of those may target the same domain, and :setting:`DOWNLOAD_DELAY` sets a "
"minimum wait between two consecutive requests to the same domain. A project "
"generated by :command:`startproject` gets one request per second per domain "
"out of these."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:131
msgid ""
"Raise them to crawl a single website faster, and see :ref:`broad-crawls-"
"concurrency` to spread requests across many websites instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:135
msgid ""
"The limit that matters, though, is the one the target website tolerates. "
"Exceeding it gets you throttled, served errors or banned, all of which make "
"the crawl slower than a lower concurrency would have been. To find that "
"limit:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:139
msgid ""
"Read the :ref:`robots.txt <topics-dlmw-robots>` file of the website. Scrapy "
"does not act on its ``Crawl-delay`` and ``Request-rate`` directives, so when "
"they are present, translate them into :setting:`DOWNLOAD_DELAY` and "
"concurrency settings yourself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:144
msgid ""
"Check the traffic that the website already gets, using a service like "
"`SimilarWeb`_ or `Cloudflare Radar`_. A rate that is a rounding error next "
"to what the website serves anyway is unlikely to be a problem for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:151
msgid ""
"Look for a documented way in. An API, a bulk export or a search endpoint is "
"both faster for you and cheaper for the website than crawling its pages, and "
"the terms of service may state a rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:155
msgid ""
"Crawl when the website is idle, in its own timezone, so that the capacity "
"you take is capacity nobody else wanted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:158
msgid ""
"Raise concurrency gradually and watch the website respond. :stat:`downloader/"
"response_status_count/{status_code}` counts for 429, 503 or the ban page of "
"the website, growing :stat:`retry/count`, or a :ref:`download latency "
"<download-latency>` that climbs as you push harder, all mean you have gone "
"past the limit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:168
msgid "Producing requests faster"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:170
msgid ""
"A spider that discovers its requests one response at a time keeps the "
"downloader idle no matter how high you set :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS`. "
"To put more requests in the scheduler earlier:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:174
msgid ""
"Request every page at once when you can work out how many there are, e.g. "
"from a page count or from a result count and a page size in the first "
"response, instead of following a link to the next page on every response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:178
msgid ""
"Get URLs from a source that lists many of them at once, such as a sitemap or "
"a search or export endpoint of the target website. For a crawl that needs "
"nothing else, :class:`~scrapy.spiders.SitemapSpider` reads sitemaps for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:183
msgid ""
"Raise the :attr:`~scrapy.Request.priority` of pagination requests, so that "
"they are downloaded before the requests that they compete with, and discover "
"the rest of the crawl sooner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:187
msgid ""
"Each of these trades memory for speed: a request produced before the "
"downloader can take it waits in the scheduler, or on disk if you set :"
"setting:`JOBDIR`. Pushed far enough, they turn memory or disk into your new "
"bottleneck, which is why :ref:`optimize-memory` recommends the reverse of "
"the last point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:196
msgid "Lowering resource usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:201
msgid "Lowering memory usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:203
msgid "Lower :setting:`SCRAPER_SLOT_MAX_ACTIVE_SIZE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:205
msgid ""
"Lower :setting:`DOWNLOAD_MAXSIZE`, which allows a single response to take up "
"to 1 GiB of memory by default, multiplied by your concurrency. Set :setting:"
"`DOWNLOAD_WARNSIZE` first to find out whether the website actually serves "
"responses that big."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:210
msgid ""
"Lower the number of :ref:`scheduled requests <topics-scheduler>` held in "
"memory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:213
msgid ""
"Increase the :attr:`~scrapy.Request.priority` of requests whose :attr:"
"`~scrapy.Request.callback` cannot yield additional requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:216
msgid ""
"For example, the following spider uses a higher priority (1) for book "
"requests than for pagination requests:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:219
msgid ""
"from scrapy import Spider\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class BooksToScrapeComSpider(Spider):\n"
"    name = \"books_toscrape_com\"\n"
"    start_urls = [\n"
"        \"http://books.toscrape.com/catalogue/category/books/mystery_3/index."
"html\"\n"
"    ]\n"
"\n"
"    def parse(self, response):\n"
"        next_page_links = response.css(\".next a\")\n"
"        yield from response.follow_all(next_page_links)\n"
"        book_links = response.css(\"article a\")\n"
"        yield from response.follow_all(book_links, callback=self.parse_book, "
"priority=1)\n"
"\n"
"    def parse_book(self, response):\n"
"        yield {\n"
"            \"name\": response.css(\"h1::text\").get(),\n"
"            \"price\": response.css(\".price_color::text\").re_first(\"£(."
"*)\"),\n"
"            \"url\": response.url,\n"
"        }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:243
msgid ""
"If the number of request-yielding, low-priority requests scheduled at any "
"given time is lower than concurrency settings (:setting:"
"`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS_PER_DOMAIN` or :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS`), as in "
"the example above, this can slow down your crawl by turning those requests "
"into a bottleneck."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:249
msgid ""
"If you have many :ref:`start requests <start-requests>`, consider :ref:"
"`delaying their iteration <start-requests-lazy>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:252
msgid "Set :setting:`JOBDIR` to offload all scheduled requests to disk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:254
msgid "Be on the lookout for :ref:`memory leaks <topics-leaks>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:258
msgid "Lowering network usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:260
msgid ""
"Enable :class:`~scrapy.downloadermiddlewares.httpcache.HttpCacheMiddleware` "
"while developing your spider, so that re-runs do not download the same "
"responses again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:266
msgid "Lowering CPU usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:268
msgid "Set :setting:`LOG_LEVEL` to ``\"INFO\"`` or higher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:270
msgid ""
"Restrict what you parse. A :ref:`selector <topics-selectors>` over a smaller "
"part of the response, or a single query whose result you reuse, beats "
"repeated queries over the whole document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:276
msgid "Other tips"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:278
msgid ""
"Try :ref:`using the asyncio reactor <install-asyncio>` with uvloop_ as :ref:"
"`custom event loop <using-custom-loops>`, i.e. setting :setting:"
"`ASYNCIO_EVENT_LOOP` to ``\"uvloop.Loop\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:284
msgid ""
"Alternatively, try :ref:`switching to a non-asyncio reactor <disable-"
"asyncio>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:287
msgid "Disable unused :ref:`components <topics-components>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:289
msgid ""
"For example, set :setting:`COOKIES_ENABLED` to ``False`` unless you need "
"cookies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:292
msgid ""
"Split the crawl across separate processes to use more than one CPU core. "
"See :ref:`distributed-crawls`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:300
msgid "Speeding up broad crawls"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:302
msgid ""
"While Scrapy is well suited for **broad crawls**, i.e. crawls that target "
"many websites, the default :ref:`settings <topics-settings>` are optimized "
"for crawls targeting a single website."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:306
msgid "For broad crawls, consider these adjustments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:310
msgid "Increase the global concurrency:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:312
msgid ""
"Set :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS` as close to :setting:"
"`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS_PER_DOMAIN` × [number of target domains] (e.g. 8 × 10 "
"domains = 80 concurrent requests) as your CPU and memory allow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:317
msgid ""
"Increase :setting:`SCRAPER_SLOT_MAX_ACTIVE_SIZE` when increasing :setting:"
"`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS` stops making a difference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:322
msgid ""
"If memory is a bottleneck, see if :ref:`crawling in BFO order <bfo>` lowers "
"memory usage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:325
msgid "Improve DNS resolution speed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:327
msgid ""
"Set up your own DNS server, with a local cache and upstream to a `large DNS "
"server`_, to avoid slowing down your network."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:332
msgid ""
"Increase :setting:`REACTOR_THREADPOOL_MAXSIZE` to the minimum value that "
"avoids DNS resolution timeouts and makes a noticeable positive impact in "
"crawl speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:336
msgid "Lower the negative impact of some responses:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:338
msgid ""
"Set :setting:`RETRY_ENABLED` to ``False`` or, if you need retries, consider "
"lowering :setting:`RETRY_TIMES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:341
msgid ""
"Lower :setting:`DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT` to a more reasonable value, to discard "
"stuck requests more quickly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/optimize.rst:344
msgid ""
"Set :setting:`REDIRECT_ENABLED` to ``False`` unless you want to follow "
"redirects."
msgstr ""
